"""Interview questions for FastAPI in Practice and PostgreSQL and SQL."""

from app.content._build import qa

INTERVIEW = {
    # FastAPI
    "fastapi-first-app": [
        qa("Why choose FastAPI over Flask or Django?",
           "FastAPI uses type hints for automatic validation, serialization and OpenAPI docs, supports async natively and is fast. Flask is minimal and flexible but needs extensions for validation and docs. Django is batteries-included (ORM, admin, auth) and suits large server-rendered sites.",
           "FastAPI is popular for JSON APIs, machine-learning model serving and microservices; Django for content-heavy sites that need an admin interface."),
        qa("What is ASGI, and how does it differ from WSGI?",
           "WSGI is the older, synchronous interface between Python web apps and servers. ASGI is its asynchronous successor and supports async code, WebSockets and long-lived connections. FastAPI is an ASGI application served by an ASGI server such as uvicorn.",
           "That's why this app starts with `uvicorn app.main:app`: uvicorn is the server and FastAPI is the application."),
    ],
    "fastapi-path-query": [
        qa("When do you use path parameters, and when query parameters?",
           "Path parameters identify a specific resource (`/users/42`). Query parameters change how a collection is returned: filtering, sorting, searching and pagination (`/users?role=admin&page=2`). Required identity goes in the path; optional options go in the query string.",
           "Designing URLs this way makes APIs predictable and cache-friendly, and matches what REST clients expect."),
        qa("How would you paginate a large collection?",
           "Offset pagination (`?page=3&size=20`) is simple but slows down on deep pages and can skip or repeat rows when data changes. Cursor (keyset) pagination returns an opaque cursor, such as the last id seen, and stays fast and stable at any depth. Always cap the page size.",
           "Feeds and activity logs with millions of rows use cursor pagination; admin tables with page numbers often use offsets."),
    ],
    "fastapi-pydantic": [
        qa("What does Pydantic do in a FastAPI app?",
           "It parses incoming JSON into typed Python objects, validates constraints (types, lengths, ranges, custom rules) and returns detailed 422 errors when validation fails. It also serializes responses and generates the JSON Schema shown in the docs.",
           "Validating at the edge means business logic can trust its inputs, which removes a whole class of defensive checks and bugs."),
        qa("Why separate request and response models?",
           "Clients shouldn't be able to set server-controlled fields such as id, created_at or role, and responses shouldn't leak internal fields such as password hashes. Separate models (often UserCreate, UserUpdate and UserOut) make each contract explicit.",
           "Returning a response model filters out sensitive fields automatically, which prevents accidental data leaks."),
    ],
    "fastapi-crud": [
        qa("What is idempotency, and which HTTP methods are idempotent?",
           "An operation is idempotent if repeating it has the same effect as doing it once. GET, PUT and DELETE are idempotent by definition; POST isn't, because each call may create a new resource. PATCH can be either, depending on the implementation.",
           "Clients can safely retry idempotent requests after a timeout; payment APIs give POST requests idempotency keys so a retry doesn't charge twice."),
        qa("Which status codes would you return for common CRUD outcomes?",
           "201 Created for a successful POST (often with a Location header), 200 OK for reads and updates that return a body, 204 No Content for deletes, 404 when the resource doesn't exist, 409 Conflict for duplicates and 422 for invalid input.",
           "Consistent status codes let frontends handle results generically, for example showing a 'not found' page for any 404."),
    ],
    "fastapi-sqlite": [
        qa("What is SQL injection, and how do you prevent it?",
           "It happens when user input is built into a SQL string and changes the query's meaning, for example to dump or delete data. Prevent it with parameterized queries (placeholders send values separately from the SQL) or an ORM that does this for you, and connect with a least-privilege database user.",
           "It's still one of the most common web vulnerabilities, so code reviews should reject any SQL built with f-strings or concatenation."),
        qa("When is SQLite a good choice, and when would you switch to PostgreSQL?",
           "SQLite is a single file with no server, excellent for small apps, prototypes, tests and single-machine tools. Switch to PostgreSQL for many concurrent writers, several app servers, advanced features (JSONB, full-text search, roles) or large data.",
           "This learning app stores one learner's progress in SQLite; a multi-user version would move to PostgreSQL, as the PostgreSQL course shows."),
    ],
    "fastapi-dependencies": [
        qa("What is dependency injection, and why is it useful?",
           "Instead of a function creating what it needs, it declares its needs and the framework supplies them. That keeps code decoupled, removes repeated setup and makes it easy to swap implementations, for example a test database instead of the real one.",
           "FastAPI endpoints receive the current user, a database session or settings through `Depends`, and tests replace them with `app.dependency_overrides`."),
        qa("How would you implement authentication with dependencies?",
           "Write a dependency that reads the token or session from the request, verifies it and returns the current user, raising 401 if it's invalid. Protected endpoints declare it as a parameter, and a second dependency built on it can check roles and raise 403.",
           "Auth logic then lives in one tested place, and protecting an endpoint takes a single extra parameter."),
    ],
    "fastapi-errors": [
        qa("What's the difference between 401 and 403?",
           "401 Unauthorized means the request isn't authenticated: credentials are missing or invalid. 403 Forbidden means the server knows who you are, but you aren't allowed to do this.",
           "A login-required page returns 401 to anonymous users, and a normal user calling an admin endpoint gets 403. This app's password prompt is a 401 with a WWW-Authenticate header."),
        qa("How should an API report errors to clients?",
           'With the right status code, a consistent JSON shape (such as `{"detail": ...}` or the RFC 9457 problem-details format) and a human-readable message, without stack traces or secrets. Log full details on the server along with a request id that the client can quote.',
           "Consistent error bodies let frontends show helpful messages, and request ids let support teams find the matching server logs."),
    ],
    "fastapi-testing": [
        qa("How do you test endpoints that depend on a database?",
           "Give each test an isolated database: a temporary SQLite file, a transaction that's rolled back after each test, or a throwaway PostgreSQL container. Inject it through an app factory or a dependency override, and seed only the data each test needs.",
           'This project\'s tests call `create_app(tmp_path / "progress.sqlite3")`, so every test starts with an empty database.'),
        qa("What would you check before deploying an API to production?",
           "Tests pass in CI; configuration and secrets come from the environment; the server runs without reload and behind HTTPS; there's a health-check endpoint; logging and error monitoring are in place; migrations and backups are planned; and timeouts and rate limits are configured.",
           "This app's Render deployment covers several of these: a Docker build, the /api/health check, secrets in environment settings and a persistent disk."),
    ],
    # PostgreSQL
    "pg-setup": [
        qa("What's the difference between a database server, a database and a schema in PostgreSQL?",
           "A server (or cluster) is one running PostgreSQL instance. It hosts several databases, which are isolated from each other: a connection belongs to exactly one. Inside a database, schemas are namespaces for tables; the default one is `public`.",
           "SaaS products sometimes give each customer their own schema or database for isolation, while simpler apps use one database with the public schema."),
        qa("Why might you choose PostgreSQL over MySQL or a NoSQL database?",
           "PostgreSQL is open source, standards-compliant and feature-rich: transactional DDL, powerful SQL (CTEs, window functions), JSONB, full-text search, strong data integrity and extensions such as PostGIS and pgvector. NoSQL stores can fit huge, simple key-value workloads, but most applications benefit from relational guarantees.",
           "Many teams use PostgreSQL as their relational store and document store at once, and pgvector even makes it a vector database for AI search."),
    ],
    "pg-tables": [
        qa("What's the difference between a primary key and a unique constraint?",
           "Both enforce uniqueness and create an index. A table has at most one primary key, its columns can't be NULL, and it's what other tables reference. A table can have many unique constraints, and they allow NULLs.",
           "A users table typically has an identity `id` primary key plus a unique `email`, so an email can change without breaking foreign keys."),
        qa("Should you use serial, identity or UUID primary keys?",
           "Identity columns are the modern, standard replacement for serial. Integer keys are compact and fast. UUIDs can be generated anywhere without a database round trip and don't reveal row counts, at the cost of larger indexes; time-ordered UUIDv7 reduces that cost.",
           "Public URLs often use UUIDs so customers can't guess other customers' ids, while internal tables keep bigint identity keys."),
    ],
    "pg-select": [
        qa("In what order does PostgreSQL logically evaluate a SELECT?",
           "FROM and JOINs, then WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING, the SELECT list (including window functions), DISTINCT, ORDER BY and finally LIMIT and OFFSET. That's why a column alias defined in SELECT can be used in ORDER BY but not in WHERE.",
           'Knowing the order explains errors such as `column "total" does not exist` when filtering on an alias, and tells you when to move logic into a subquery or CTE.'),
        qa("How does NULL behave in SQL?",
           "NULL means unknown, so comparisons with it (=, <>, <) produce NULL, which WHERE treats as false. Use `IS NULL`, `IS DISTINCT FROM` or `coalesce()` to handle it. Aggregates other than `count(*)` ignore NULLs.",
           "`WHERE status <> 'cancelled'` silently drops rows whose status is NULL, a common reporting bug; `IS DISTINCT FROM` keeps them."),
    ],
    "pg-modify": [
        qa("What is an upsert, and how do you write one in PostgreSQL?",
           "An upsert inserts a row, or updates it if it already exists, in one atomic statement. PostgreSQL uses `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (columns) DO UPDATE SET ...` (or DO NOTHING), which requires a unique constraint or index on the conflict columns.",
           "Syncing records from an external API, counting page views per day and saving user settings are typical upserts that stay correct under concurrent requests."),
        qa("What's the difference between DELETE, TRUNCATE and DROP?",
           "DELETE removes rows that match a WHERE clause, one by one, and fires triggers. TRUNCATE removes all rows at once, much faster. DROP removes the table itself, structure included. All three are transactional in PostgreSQL, so they can be rolled back inside a transaction.",
           "Test suites often TRUNCATE tables between tests; production code uses DELETE with a WHERE clause, and DROP belongs only in migrations."),
    ],
    "pg-aggregate": [
        qa("What's the difference between WHERE and HAVING?",
           "WHERE filters individual rows before grouping and can't use aggregates. HAVING filters groups after aggregation and can, for example `HAVING count(*) > 10`. Put non-aggregate conditions in WHERE so fewer rows have to be grouped.",
           "A report of customers who spent more than 1,000 this year filters the year in WHERE and the total in HAVING."),
        qa("How would you calculate a conditional count, such as paid and unpaid orders per customer?",
           "Use aggregate FILTER clauses, such as `count(*) FILTER (WHERE status = 'paid')`, or `sum(CASE WHEN ... THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)` in databases without FILTER. One pass over the data then returns several metrics side by side.",
           "Dashboards compute conversion rates, error counts and daily totals in a single grouped query this way."),
    ],
    "pg-joins": [
        qa("Explain INNER, LEFT, RIGHT and FULL OUTER joins.",
           "INNER keeps only matching rows. LEFT keeps every row from the left table, with NULLs where the right table has no match; RIGHT is the mirror image. FULL OUTER keeps every row from both sides. CROSS JOIN pairs every row with every other row.",
           "Listing all customers with their orders, including customers with none, is a LEFT JOIN; reconciling two systems' records to find mismatches on either side is a FULL OUTER JOIN."),
        qa("What is the N+1 query problem?",
           "Loading a list with one query and then running one more query per row to fetch related data, giving N+1 round trips. Replace it with a JOIN, an `IN (...)` query or the ORM's eager loading.",
           "A page listing 100 posts with their authors can drop from 101 queries to one; it's one of the most common ORM performance problems."),
    ],
    "pg-schema-design": [
        qa("What is normalization, and when would you denormalize?",
           "Normalization organises data so each fact is stored once, which prevents update anomalies; third normal form is the usual target. Denormalize deliberately, by storing a copied or computed value, when reads vastly outnumber writes and joins are a measured bottleneck, and keep the copy in sync.",
           "An order stores the price at the time of purchase (a deliberate copy), while product names stay normalized; analytics tables are often denormalized for speed."),
        qa("How do you model a many-to-many relationship?",
           "With a junction table holding two foreign keys, usually with a composite primary key across both, plus any attributes of the relationship itself, such as the date someone enrolled or their role.",
           "Students and courses, users and groups, and products and tags are all modelled this way."),
    ],
    "pg-ctes": [
        qa("CTE or subquery: is there a performance difference?",
           "Since PostgreSQL 12, simple non-recursive CTEs referenced once are inlined like subqueries, so performance is usually the same. `WITH ... AS MATERIALIZED` forces the CTE to be computed once, and `NOT MATERIALIZED` forces inlining.",
           "Use CTEs for readability by default, and check EXPLAIN when a heavy CTE is referenced several times."),
        qa("Where would you use a recursive CTE?",
           "For any hierarchy or graph stored as parent links: category trees, org charts, threaded comments, bills of materials or a package's transitive dependencies. Add a depth limit or a cycle check when the data might contain loops.",
           "An online shop builds breadcrumbs such as 'Electronics > Laptops > Gaming' by walking up the category tree with WITH RECURSIVE."),
    ],
    "pg-window": [
        qa("What's the difference between GROUP BY and window functions?",
           "GROUP BY collapses rows into one per group. A window function computes a value across related rows but keeps every row, so you can show each row's detail next to group totals, ranks or running sums.",
           "A sales report that shows each order next to the customer's total uses `sum(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id)`."),
        qa("How do you get the latest row per group, such as each user's most recent login?",
           "Number the rows with `row_number() OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY logged_in_at DESC)` in a CTE and keep the rows numbered 1. PostgreSQL also offers `SELECT DISTINCT ON (user_id) ... ORDER BY user_id, logged_in_at DESC`.",
           "'Latest status per order', 'current price per product' and 'most recent message per conversation' are all this pattern."),
    ],
    "pg-jsonb": [
        qa("What's the difference between json and jsonb?",
           "json stores the exact input text, including whitespace and duplicate keys, and is re-parsed on every access. jsonb stores a decomposed binary form that's faster to query, supports indexing and containment operators, and drops duplicate keys and key order. Prefer jsonb.",
           "Webhook payloads, user preferences and product attributes that vary by category are common uses of jsonb columns."),
        qa("What are the risks of putting too much data into JSONB?",
           "You lose column types, constraints and foreign keys, queries become harder to read, and the planner has weaker statistics. Updating one key rewrites the whole value.",
           "A good rule: core fields you filter, join or constrain on become real columns, and genuinely variable extras go in jsonb."),
    ],
    "pg-indexes": [
        qa("How does a B-tree index speed up a query, and when won't PostgreSQL use it?",
           "A B-tree keeps keys sorted in a shallow, balanced tree, so finding a value or a range takes a few page reads instead of a full table scan. The planner may ignore it when a query matches a large share of rows, when a function wraps the column (such as `lower(email)`) without a matching expression index, or when the leading columns of a composite index aren't filtered.",
           "Case-insensitive login lookups need an index on `lower(email)`; otherwise every login scans the whole users table."),
        qa("How would you investigate a slow query?",
           "Run `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` to see the real plan and the time spent in each step. Look for sequential scans on large tables, row estimates far from the actual counts and expensive sorts. Then add or adjust an index, rewrite the query or refresh statistics with ANALYZE, and measure again. pg_stat_statements shows which queries cost the most overall.",
           "Teams usually start from pg_stat_statements in production to find the queries with the highest total time, and tune those first."),
    ],
    "pg-transactions": [
        qa("What does ACID mean?",
           "Atomicity: a transaction's changes all happen or none do. Consistency: constraints hold before and after it. Isolation: concurrent transactions don't see each other's unfinished work. Durability: once committed, data survives crashes, thanks to the write-ahead log.",
           "Moving money between accounts, placing an order while reducing stock, or creating a user together with their profile must be atomic, or data ends up half-updated."),
        qa("What is MVCC, and how do the isolation levels differ?",
           "PostgreSQL uses multi-version concurrency control: writers create new row versions, so readers never block writers and writers never block readers. READ COMMITTED (the default) gives each statement a fresh snapshot; REPEATABLE READ gives one snapshot for the whole transaction; SERIALIZABLE also detects anomalies and aborts a transaction, which the app then retries.",
           "Reports can run during heavy writes without blocking them, and financial logic often uses SERIALIZABLE with automatic retries."),
    ],
    "pg-python": [
        qa("Why use a connection pool, and how do you size it?",
           "Each new PostgreSQL connection costs a server process and several milliseconds, so a pool keeps a few connections open for reuse. Keep pools small, roughly a few connections per CPU core of the database server, because too many connections waste memory and cause contention. With many app instances, an external pooler such as PgBouncer helps.",
           "A FastAPI service with four workers might use a pool of five per worker; serverless apps that open many short-lived connections rely on PgBouncer or a managed pooler."),
        qa("ORM or raw SQL: what are the trade-offs?",
           "ORMs (SQLAlchemy, the Django ORM) map tables to classes, reduce boilerplate, help with migrations and parameterize queries, but can hide inefficient queries such as N+1. Raw SQL gives full control over complex queries and PostgreSQL-specific features. Many teams use an ORM for everyday CRUD and raw SQL for reports.",
           "Whichever you choose, log or inspect the generated SQL during development so performance problems show up early."),
    ],
    "pg-operations": [
        qa("How would you change a production schema without downtime?",
           "Use backward-compatible steps: add new columns as nullable or with a default, deploy code that writes to both the old and new columns, backfill in batches, switch reads, and drop the old column in a later release. Create indexes with `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY` so writes aren't blocked.",
           "Renaming a column in one step breaks the running app; this 'expand and contract' pattern lets old and new code run side by side during a deploy."),
        qa("What does a good backup strategy look like?",
           "Regular full backups (pg_dump for small databases, physical base backups for large ones) plus continuous write-ahead-log archiving for point-in-time recovery. Keep encrypted copies off the server, and restore them regularly to prove they work and to measure how long recovery takes.",
           "Managed services such as Render, Amazon RDS and Cloud SQL automate snapshots and point-in-time recovery, but you still need to know how to restore and to practise it."),
    ],
}
